{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792853478,"nonce":"AkhO+GxnBV9tcV+E9NyeK89IOmE=","title":"OSR: an inner loop entered through its helper restarts from 0 when the outer loop comes round","message":"A Python permutation loop (`while n:` around `for i in range(r - 1, -1, -1): ... break`) gave wrong results on the LLVM tier in the one call whose frame moved into the LLVM OSR helper at the inner loop's header: n_queens over 8 returned 4, 11 or 13 solutions instead of 92 in one of fifteen iterations, and a single call over 10 returned 0 instead of 724 about half the time (always with ZYNTAX_OSR_TRACE=1). Cranelift alone, ZYNTAX_DISABLE_OSR=1 and the interpreter were right; so was the same loop written with `while`.\n\nosr_layout_with skipped the header's own phi incomings when collecting live-ins, on the reading that they are either back edges or loop-entry values the frame supersedes. Here the inner loop's entry edge comes from a block inside the region (the outer loop leads back to it), and its value, `r - 1` computed before both loops, was read again the next time round. It was never in the frame, and the LLVM helper seeds every instruction value with zero before materialising, so the inner loop restarted at 0, ran out without its break, and the function returned early. Such a value's definition dominates the header (it is outside the region yet dominates a block in it), so the probe always has it.\n\nNote for later: the zero seeding in compile_osr_helper turns any value a layout misses into a silent 0 instead of a refused helper.\n\nReproducer: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-amaterasu-Vibranium-zyntax/56183c5f-5acc-4cc6-9954-39174de9c11a/scratchpad/red/r3.py 10 under ZYPY_LLVM=1 ZYNTAX_OSR_TRACE=1 (expected 3628799).","files":null}]}